<h1>Colophon</h1>
<p class="lede">Provenance, privacy, design, and how this site keeps itself honest.</p>

<h2>Provenance</h2>
<p>This is the public reading surface of <em>A Systems Theory for Today</em>, a research program run as <strong>designed disagreement</strong>: a standing panel of reconstructed thinkers argues; <strong>Claude</strong> (Anthropic) chairs — preparing the ground, synthesizing the argument, and never casting a vote; <strong>Hulki Okan Tabak</strong> is the author, who directs the program and ratifies its outputs. Nothing is load-bearing until the author ratifies it, and no genuinely-argued position is ever silently erased. The private workshop repository holds every panel session, catch, dissent, and decision that led here.</p>
<p>The website renders the repository's own Markdown in place — the repository <em>is</em> the site's source. It is built with <a href="https://www.11ty.dev/">Eleventy</a> and deployed to GitHub Pages; the reading site is public while the contribution repository stays private until the skeleton is stable.</p>

<h2>Privacy &amp; analytics</h2>
<p>There are no accounts, no cookies, and no tracking. The only analytics is <strong>GoatCounter</strong>, a privacy-respecting counter that records an anonymous page view — no personal data, no fingerprinting, no cross-site tracking — and is simply absent when you are offline or on localhost. A strict Content-Security-Policy names the handful of hosts this site may talk to (its own origin, Google Fonts, the Mermaid diagram library, and the counter) and forbids everything else; the counter's script is pinned to a published version by cryptographic hash, so even that host cannot serve this page altered code. IP addresses are used only transiently to count a visit and are not stored.</p>

<h2>Design</h2>
<p>Typeset in <strong>Newsreader</strong> (a screen-first serif) with <strong>IBM Plex Mono</strong> for labels and metadata, on a deep indigo field by night and a warm cream field by day — either way with a single amber accent and a cyan second for what is contested. The nine <a href="{{ '/diagrams/' | url }}">figures</a> are the project's signature: eight authored in <a href="https://mermaid.js.org/">Mermaid</a> (which live as editable text in the repository and render on GitHub) and one hand-authored SVG; every figure is downloadable as vector (SVG) or raster (PNG). The home-screen icon is the project's own signed causal loop. If a coupling can't be drawn, we probably don't understand it yet.</p>

<h2>How it keeps itself honest</h2>
<p>Empirical claims are grounded in sources; the panel's own inferences and guesses are labelled as such; overturned claims are marked overturned rather than deleted. The unsettled questions and standing disagreements are a first-class part of the document — kept, curated, and pointed to as <a href="{{ '/logs/open_questions/' | url }}">the frontier</a>. The project runs a metabolism that turns error into rule change (<a href="{{ '/logs/catches/' | url }}">catches</a> → <a href="{{ '/logs/learnings/' | url }}">learnings</a> → amended ground rules) and keeps an honest <a href="{{ '/logs/reflections/' | url }}">retrospective</a> on whether the method is actually working. The historical and contemporary surveys carry their own sources; see the <a href="{{ '/history/' | url }}">history</a> and its full <a href="{{ '/docs/history_of_systems_theories/' | url }}">record</a>.</p>

<h2>License</h2>
<p>Documents (the prose, the theories, the surveys) are licensed <strong>CC BY-SA 4.0</strong> — share and adapt, with attribution, under the same terms. Any code is <strong>MIT</strong>. It is meant to be forked, contradicted, and extended once the repository opens.</p>

